# Line endings — the reason `pnpm format` used to rewrite the whole repo.
#
# Git on Windows is commonly configured with core.autocrlf=true, which checks
# every text file out with CRLF. `.prettierrc` asks for `"endOfLine": "lf"`.
# With nothing to reconcile the two, prettier saw CRLF everywhere and reported
# all ~500 tracked text files as unformatted — permanently. Running
# `prettier --write` "fixed" them, git converted them straight back on the next
# checkout, and the next person to run `pnpm format` got 80+ files of churn
# mixed into whatever they were actually changing.
#
# This settles it in git's favour of LF, in the repo AND in the working tree,
# which is what prettier, the Linux build and the deployed server all expect.
# `text=auto` still detects binaries and leaves them alone.
#
# There are no .bat or .cmd files in this repo; if one is ever added it needs
# `eol=crlf`, because cmd.exe will not run an LF-terminated batch file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Supply-house exports end their lines in CRLF, and the price-sheet fixtures
# are generated that way ON PURPOSE (scripts/makePriceSheetFixture.mts) so the
# parser is tested against the bytes a real export has. Stored byte-exact:
# the LF rule above would quietly convert them on the next commit and every
# fresh clone would test the easy case instead (found 2026-09-25).
server/fixtures/*.csv -text
server/fixtures/*.tsv -text

# Declared rather than left to detection, so a corrupt diff can never be
# attempted on them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.xlsx binary
